    {
      "@id": "#claim",
      "@type": "Claim",
      "name": "Entry 24: what the literature says a cumulative-ascent measurement is worth — and why our energy endpoint can decide what geometry cannot",
      "text": "The survey brackets our measurements: consumer-baro h₊ error is ~1–5% device-level, DEM per-point accuracy does NOT transfer to accumulated ascent, and the consensus direction is GPS-only under-reads while DEM correction over-reads — Entries 6 and 19 sit inside every located band, and Entry 19's flat-terrain FABDEM failure is sharper than anything located. The decidability point: h₊ has no true value, only a value at a smoothing scale, so geometry alone cannot adjudicate between sources — the measured-energy endpoint turns the smoothing scale into a fittable parameter (Entries 19–21), a validation no located study performs.",
